RECON — automated improvement log
=================================
One entry per scheduled run: what changed, and what's worth picking up next.


2026-07-24 — Empty states, loading skeletons, responsive shell, line-ending fix
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
First automated run, so it started by clearing two things that would have
taxed every run after it.

Housekeeping first (two separate commits, kept apart from the feature work):
  * Checkpointed pre-existing uncommitted work found in the tree — Backup
    promoted from a modal to a full rail view, tray master/client discovery,
    Chrome/Edge app-mode window — so there was a rollback point before
    touching anything.
  * Added .gitattributes and normalized static/ to LF. The four static files
    were saved CRLF against LF-stored blobs, so each one diffed as a total
    rewrite: app.js showed ~5,900 changed lines for ~115 lines of real edits.
    This run's feature diff is 368 lines and actually reviewable as a result.
    Also un-ignored auto_improvements.log, which the blanket *.log rule in
    .gitignore was silently swallowing.

Bug fixed:
  * wireVehiclesView() bound its filter chips with the global selector
    ".filters .chip", which matched every chip in the app — the A/P date
    ranges, cores, returns, and task filters. Clicking any of those also ran
    the vehicles handler, wiping the active state off every chip on every
    screen and setting state.filter to undefined. Now scoped to
    "#view-vehicles .filters .chip", matching how every other view already
    did it.

UI work:
  * Empty states: one emptyState()/emptyRow() component replaced ~14 hand-
    rolled inline-styled one-liners. Each list now distinguishes "nothing
    added yet" (with a button that starts the thing) from "your search or
    filter hid it all" (with a way to clear it) instead of a flat "No
    vehicles match." Covers vehicles, A/P, cores, returns, staff, backups,
    tasks, suggestions, vendors, notes, activity, deposits, estimate lines,
    and report output. Failed loads render in the critical colour so they
    aren't mistaken for an ordinary empty list.
  * Loading skeletons: showView() now paints shimmer rows before the fetch
    starts. Previously the old view's rows sat on screen until the request
    resolved, so the app looked like it had ignored the click.
  * Responsive: the shell was a fixed 236px rail and a hardcoded 4-column
    stats grid. Added breakpoints — stats reflow at 1240/700, the rail
    narrows at 1100 and collapses to an icon strip at 900, filters and topbar
    wrap. Matters more than it looks like it does now that the tray opens the
    UI in a resizable app-mode window.
  * Focus rings via :focus-visible (buttons, chips, rail items and rows had
    none — keyboard nav was invisible), themed scrollbars, and the topbar
    shows the shop name again via the .shop-name rule that had been sitting
    in the stylesheet unused. Dropped the dead .shop-acronym rule.

Tests: added tests/test_static_assets.py (5 tests) — cross-checks that every
$("#id") in app.js resolves to an element in index.html (259 references
checked), that rail buttons point at views that exist, that referenced
/assets/ files are present, that no id is declared twice, and that new empty
states go through the shared component. There's no build step for this
front-end, so nothing else catches a renamed element; a dead selector just
silently returns null. All five were mutation-tested to confirm they fail
when the thing they check is broken.

Suite: 155 -> 160 passing. Server boots clean; /, /assets/*, /api/health and
/api/dashboard all verified over real HTTP.

Next time, in rough priority order:
  1. The vehicle detail page is the densest screen and still the least
     polished — the parts/labor grid is an 11-column CSS grid with hardcoded
     fr units that will overflow badly under ~1100px, where the rest of the
     app now reflows. It needs a real responsive treatment or a horizontal
     scroll container.
  2. renderEstimate() and friends re-bind every row's listeners on every
     render. Event delegation (as now used for the empty-state buttons)
     would cut a lot of that and fix the subtle focus-loss on re-render.
  3. No client-side error boundary: a throw inside a render function leaves
     a half-drawn screen with no message. A try/catch at the view level that
     renders the error empty-state variant would make failures legible.
  4. Toasts are the only feedback channel and they auto-dismiss at 3.2s —
     destructive actions (void, delete, restore) deserve a confirm pattern
     better than window.confirm().
  5. Consider a real front-end smoke test (Playwright) — the static checks
     added this run catch dead selectors but nothing exercises a click path.

================================================================================
2026-07-25 -- Parts & Labor grid rebuild, in-app confirm dialog, error boundary,
              and a real front-end smoke test
================================================================================

Picked up items 1, 3, 4 and 5 from last run's list.

Parts & Labor grid (the densest screen in the app, and the least polished):
  * The row was a hardcoded 11/12-column CSS grid of bare inputs whose header
    row was display:none inside .parts-labor-shell. So Qty, Cost and Core were
    unlabelled number boxes, and below ~1100px -- exactly where the rest of the
    app now reflows -- the tracks squeezed to slivers.
  * Every field now sits in a .pr-cell wrapper carrying a data-label. Wide, the
    wrappers are grid tracks under a header row that's visible again; narrow,
    the same markup reflows into a stacked card with the data-label as each
    field's caption. No second template, no duplicated markup.
  * The header row doubles as each section's caption: inside a job's Parts
    block the first column reads "Parts" instead of "Kind", which let the
    separate .kind-subgroup-label row go away entirely. In stacked mode the
    header collapses to just that word; the flat list's header (which only ever
    said "Kind") hides, since every field labels itself down there.
  * Breakpoints are @container, not @media -- the width this grid actually gets
    depends on whether the details drawer is open beside it, which a media
    query can't see. Two tiers: tighten at 1040px, stack at 820px. There's an
    @supports fallback on viewport width for engines without container queries.
  * Qty/Cost/Core/Part# are monospace with tabular numerals and right-aligned,
    so a column of costs actually lines up on the decimal.
  * addEstimateRow()'s transient "Saving..." row emits the same cell scaffolding
    (including empty spacers) -- it used to render 7 bare children into an
    11-column grid and sat visibly misaligned until the save round-tripped.

Confirm dialog:
  * 15 destructive actions went through window.confirm(), which renders OS
    chrome: no title/detail split, no way to mark an action as destructive, and
    in the app-mode window it looks like it came from a different program.
  * confirmAction({title, body, confirmLabel, danger}) is an async drop-in
    backed by the same <dialog> element as every other modal. Destructive
    variants get a red hairline, a red eyebrow, a .btn-danger confirm, and
    default focus on Cancel so hammering Enter can't delete anything.
  * Every call site was rewritten to say what will actually happen -- "Delete
    R-1042?" now explains that History is the non-destructive option, the
    restore prompts explain that the current DB is saved aside first, etc.

Error boundary:
  * showView()'s if-chain became VIEW_LOADERS + runViewLoader(), which catches
    anything a loader throws and paints renderViewFailure() into that view's
    own container (reusing the VIEW_PLACEHOLDERS map) with the message and a
    Try Again button. Previously a throw mid-render left the loading skeletons
    frozen on screen forever with only a console error.
  * The loaders' own catch blocks were toasting and returning -- same frozen
    skeletons. They now render the failure state too.
  * window.onerror / unhandledrejection report to the console and a toast, so a
    bug that escapes everything at least says the app is in a bad state instead
    of looking merely slow.

Tests: 160 -> 167.
  * test_static_assets.py gained 5: the estimate header row must have the same
    cells in the same order as its data rows; --pr-cols must declare exactly as
    many tracks as there are cells (both the flat and .has-jobs variants); every
    cell needs a .pr-<name> rule; no raw window.confirm() may come back; every
    view with a loader needs a placeholder target so the boundary has somewhere
    to draw. All mutation-tested.
  * tests/dom/estimate_smoke.mjs + tests/test_dom_smoke.py: the front end now
    actually *runs* in a test. jsdom boots the real index.html, evaluates the
    real app.js, fires DOMContentLoaded, then drives renderEstimate() (flat and
    job-grouped), addEstimateRow(), collectEstimateItems(), confirmAction()
    (accept/cancel/Esc) and renderViewFailure(). Needs node + jsdom, neither of
    which the app depends on, so it skips cleanly when they're absent
    (`cd tests/dom && npm install jsdom` to turn it on).

Suite green with and without jsdom; server boots clean and /, /assets/*,
/api/health, /api/dashboard and /api/vehicles-board were all verified over real
HTTP.

Next time, in rough priority order:
  1. Event delegation for estimate rows. renderEstimate() still re-binds every
     row's listeners on every render, which is why editing a field and having
     the save round-trip steals focus. One delegated listener on
     #vd-estimate-items would fix the focus loss and cut a lot of work per
     keystroke.
  2. The confirm dialog is the pattern; now use it for the remaining
     irreversible things that currently just do it (bulk status changes, the
     archive/reopen toggle on a single row).
  3. Extend the jsdom smoke test to the vehicles board and A/P screens -- the
     harness exists now, adding a screen is ~20 lines.
  4. The details drawer is remembered globally, but its right place is probably
     per-screen-width: it should start closed on a narrow window instead of
     forcing the estimate grid into stacked mode on a wide monitor.
  5. Toasts still auto-dismiss at 3.2s with no history. An error toast that
     scrolls past unread is gone for good; a small log behind the topbar would
     make failures recoverable.

Note for the next run: this session's sandbox can rename but not unlink files
under .git/, so git leaves .git/index.lock and .git/HEAD.lock behind after
every command and the *next* git command refuses to start. If that happens,
`mv .git/index.lock .git/stale-tmp/` (rather than rm) clears it. Stray
.git/objects/*/tmp_obj_* files land in .git/stale-tmp/ for the same reason --
that directory is safe to delete from Windows.
